WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.227
At the bakery counter, you can order a cake for Saturday.

2
00:00:06.227 --> 00:00:10.763
You fill in a form: one chocolate cake, two layers, happy birthday on top.

3
00:00:10.763 --> 00:00:12.908
Nothing gets baked while you write.

4
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.543
You can use your very best handwriting.

5
00:00:15.543 --> 00:00:16.432
Still no cake.

6
00:00:16.432 --> 00:00:19.860
Baking starts when you sign the form and hand it over.

7
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.099
And if you never do, the oven never hears about it.

8
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.616
Reactor, the library this track uses from here on, works the same way.

9
00:00:28.616 --> 00:00:30.474
It has two kinds of stream.

10
00:00:30.474 --> 00:00:33.501
A Flux is many items, like a tray of loaves.

11
00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:36.391
A Mono is one item or none, like one cake.

12
00:00:36.391 --> 00:00:39.556
Both are publishers, the ovens from last time.

13
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.493
Now, a question many of us are too shy to ask.

14
00:00:43.493 --> 00:00:47.069
In Java, a line of code does its work when it runs.

15
00:00:47.069 --> 00:00:50.646
So once the line with map has run, map has run too.

16
00:00:50.646 --> 00:00:51.067
Right?

17
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.685
Let's test it.

18
00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:57.801
Flux.range gives us the numbers 1, 2 and 3: three prices in euros.

19
00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:00.980
map turns each one into cents, times 100.

20
00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.088
And inside map, a counter goes up every time it runs.

21
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:06.776
The line has finished.

22
00:01:06.776 --> 00:01:08.629
How many times has map run?

23
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:12.025
Zero.

24
00:01:12.025 --> 00:01:13.961
map hasn't lifted a finger.

25
00:01:13.961 --> 00:01:16.828
Building the chain ran none of our code.

26
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.868
Here's what happened instead.

27
00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:26.661
An operator, a method like map that adds one step, writes one more line on the order form, and hands you back a new form.

28
00:01:26.661 --> 00:01:31.298
So map wrapped the range in a new object, a FluxMap holding a FluxRange.

29
00:01:31.298 --> 00:01:33.939
Our lambda is kept on the form for later.

30
00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:35.871
And the form isn't signed yet.

31
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.500
Now we sign.

32
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.181
We call subscribe, and watch which way things travel.

33
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.398
Our subscriber subscribes to map.

34
00:01:43.398 --> 00:01:47.089
map makes a subscriber of its own, and subscribes to range.

35
00:01:47.089 --> 00:01:51.217
So the signature travels up the chain, from our end to the source.

36
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:54.971
Then the slip from last time, request, goes up the same way.

37
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:57.638
Only now does the baking start.

38
00:01:57.638 --> 00:02:00.708
range sends 1, 2 and 3 down the chain.

39
00:02:00.708 --> 00:02:05.393
map runs once for each, and we get 100, 200 and 300 cents.

40
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:06.776
And the counter?

41
00:02:06.776 --> 00:02:07.242
Three.

42
00:02:07.242 --> 00:02:10.503
Zero before the signature, three after it.

43
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:12.331
These moments have names.

44
00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:14.462
Filling in the form is assembly.

45
00:02:14.462 --> 00:02:19.190
Signing it is subscription: the chain links up, and the demand goes up.

46
00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.855
Then the data flows down.

47
00:02:20.855 --> 00:02:23.052
Three passes over the same chain.

48
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.177
So the rule is: building a chain runs nothing.

49
00:02:26.177 --> 00:02:28.312
Only a subscribe starts the work.

50
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:31.493
Here's a trap that follows from it.

51
00:02:31.493 --> 00:02:34.473
Three orders come in: 5 loaves, 50 and 500.

52
00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:39.464
We only want the big ones, so we call filter, and keep the ones over 10.

53
00:02:39.464 --> 00:02:41.682
Then we subscribe to the orders.

54
00:02:41.682 --> 00:02:42.860
Which ones print?

55
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:47.589
All three, the 5 included.

56
00:02:47.589 --> 00:02:51.960
filter wrote a new form with the change on it, and nobody kept it.

57
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.417
We signed the old one.

58
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.627
It compiles, it runs, and it filters nothing.

59
00:02:56.627 --> 00:02:58.713
The quietest bug in the bakery.

60
00:02:58.713 --> 00:03:03.221
So the point is: an operator never changes the form you call it on.

61
00:03:03.221 --> 00:03:04.768
It hands you a new one.

62
00:03:04.768 --> 00:03:06.988
Subscribe to what filter returns.

63
00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:08.321
One more.

64
00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:13.677
If a signature starts the baking, what happens when the same form is signed twice?

65
00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:18.057
The lesson's ColdDemo has a Mono that bakes a cake for order-42.

66
00:03:18.057 --> 00:03:21.200
The code subscribes once, to write the shop log.

67
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.510
Then it calls block.

68
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:28.272
block is a subscribe too: it waits for the cake and hands it back, here to the customer.

69
00:03:34.933 --> 00:03:35.636
Two cakes.

70
00:03:35.636 --> 00:03:37.813
The shop log wrote down cake-1.

71
00:03:37.813 --> 00:03:40.271
The customer went home with cake-2.

72
00:03:40.271 --> 00:03:42.448
And the flour bill covers both.

73
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.750
Somewhere, an accountant is asking why one birthday needed two cakes.

74
00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.216
And here's where the form picture breaks.

75
00:03:49.216 --> 00:03:52.103
A clerk handed the same form twice would notice.

76
00:03:52.103 --> 00:03:53.246
Reactor never does.

77
00:03:53.246 --> 00:03:57.396
Every subscribe is a fresh signature, and the whole order runs again.

78
00:03:57.533 --> 00:04:02.529
A publisher that does its work again for every subscriber is called cold.

79
00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:03.693
Most of them are.

80
00:04:03.693 --> 00:04:06.773
The fix is one chain, with the log inside it.

81
00:04:06.773 --> 00:04:09.784
doOnNext writes the log as the cake goes by.

82
00:04:09.784 --> 00:04:12.521
Then one block: one signature, one cake.

83
00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:15.261
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

84
00:04:15.261 --> 00:04:18.120
The first statement only fills in the form.

85
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.375
The subscribe line signs it, and only then do range and map run.

86
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.869
So, has map run once its line has run?

87
00:04:25.869 --> 00:04:26.085
No.

88
00:04:26.085 --> 00:04:27.884
A chain is an order form.

89
00:04:27.884 --> 00:04:33.571
Subscribe signs it, and every signature on a cold publisher bakes a fresh cake.

90
00:04:33.733 --> 00:04:34.528
Quick check.

91
00:04:34.528 --> 00:04:38.636
Our prices chain, with three items, is subscribed three times.

92
00:04:38.636 --> 00:04:41.220
How many times does map's function run?

93
00:04:44.267 --> 00:04:44.684
Nine.

94
00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:47.608
Three items, in each of three runs.
